{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1501019-little-help-with-uncommon-spirits","id":1501019,"title":"Little help with uncommon spirits","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-01-22T04:28:09-08:00","id":1501019,"title":"Little help with uncommon spirits","content":"My pc are going in our next scenario to go exploring the umbra, looking for old/forgotten spirits. One of them want to specialized in fetiche creation and he had a few idea of fetiche that he could create with the help of time, death, musical spirits and other less known or less common spirits. Time spirit to learn gift allowing him to manipulate time or create a fetiche to do so, death to get some deadly powers, and music to create fetiche that can regenerate willpower of those who listen (or heal mental illness, temporarly increase maximum rage of garou, stun ennemy...).<br /> <br /> As the book does not (and simply cannot) state all powers that it's possible to get or obtain through fetiche i need help to get some idea about how to represent those spirit (like is the death spirit will present as the great reaper with a dark and ghostly voice or simply through a cold temperature and deep sensation calm and/or sadness?) How would they react to the pack, their behavior. What would they ask as a chiminage to join a Cairn<br /> <br /> As extension about that fetiche crafting part i wonder how frequent it is to create a fetiche among the different sept/tribes. I know that it's something important but as you must convince the spirit to give up it's freedom i have a hard time picturing myself how many fetiche you can find in a sept, how powerfull they are and how many a garou can carry some.<br /> On character creation a pc can choose to get his fetiche historical to 5 and take 5 rank 1 fetiche without any other rule limitation than the story of the character hismelf and the st needs and recommendation, and the other pc can do the same, getting a pack with potentially 5 to 10 fetiche...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26839,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","user":"Cain Loup-Noir","date":". 01-22-2023, 07:32 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-01-22T11:47:47-08:00","id":1501043,"title":"","content":"The lower the level of the fetish, the more common it is. This means that 5 minor fetishes are much easier to find, and that's why you can easily buy them for the background. This is for the simple reason that lower level fetishes are powered by lower level spirits, who are much easier to persuade into entering and staying within a fetish. The more complicated the power of the fetish, you will need a higher order of spirit inside it, and the higher order of spirit will be less cooperative and demand more out of the people making the fetishes.<br /> <br /> How often a certain sept crafts fetishes depends on the sept itself:<ul><li>how many craftsman it has on hand (to produce the high quality items needed to make fetishes)</li> <li>the reputation of the sept and their Caern among the spirit and how easy it is to get them to &quot;sign up&quot;</li> <li>the amount of Theurges with time and interest to make fetishes</li> </ul>Making a fetish is a commitment in time and resources and one that's not undertaken lightly. It's not like going to a store and buying a tool, it's literally about building the perfect tool that resonates with the spirit you want to house within to perform a certain task that you might not be able to. Plus, the spirit has to sign up to stay for a very long time in that item and persuading it will take a toll on the craftsman.<br /> <br /> I'm sorry it's not an exact answer, as a lot will depend on the context, but it does explain the logistics of fetishes. I recommend &quot;Hammer and Klaive&quot; for more information on Fetish making and Fetish culture in the Nation.<br /> <br /> For all three of your spirit examples, they don't have a specific look. They embody the idea and don't even have to have a physical shape, or they just might.And keep in mind that they are not just one spirit, there are many different ones.<br /> <br /> Time and Music spirits aren't usually close allies to the Garou, as they aren't nature spirits. They belong to the Astral Realm and as concepts will be quite alien in their communication and expression. This doesn't mean that the Garou cannot interact with them, but these spirits don't have a natural predilection to dealing with the Garou, so they will have to tread carefuly and make sure they are understood in their requests and that they understand exactly what these alien entities might want from them (High Enigmas would be quite beneficial here, and High Performance might be crucial in communicating with the spirits of Music). What would they ask for? Time would ask for time, possibly taking the time from the Pack or giving them more time than they bargained for. Music would seek to spread around the world and find new people to play it and venues that would perform in its glory.<br /> <br /> Death spirits on the other hand are natural spirits, but not ones that even the Garou would be happy to interact with. They have their own realms that can be reached from the Middle Umbra and the realms are defined by the specific interpretation of the Death spirit. They are the spirits of the idea of Death. Passing from life to not-life. They can look like anything the people seeing them expect death to be, or they can be simple things, like the sound of a dirge, a lost mourner, a long past friend or family members, a cold wind that takes the warmth away or a laconic skeleton that TALKS IN ALL CAPS. They are liminal beings that don't bring death, they just are the embodiment of death. They make those around them uneasy, and usually people traffic with them to get into the Dark Umbra, speak with Ancestors, bring peace to the dying or the undead. If you seek spirits of killing, you will be better served by spirits of Murder or War. Yet some of them may be willing to teach you the ways of inflicting harm on others, you might not be happy while associating with those, however.<br /> <br /> Axis Mundi has an example Death spirit - The Maidens of Styx, their typicial taboos say that the bearer of their fetishes can NEVER aid in healing of Garou or their allies. For their service they typically ask for &quot;freeing&quot; someone from a place of undeath, and that you must never ever meet their gaze when you met with them.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-01-22T14:38:21-08:00","id":1501063,"title":"","content":"The issue of how many fetishes are out there, the association between Garou rank and fetishes possessed, the ease and frequency of creation, how long fetishes last before being lost or destroyed, and other economic/demographic issues is really one that every ST needs to decide for himself. There's relatively little guidance in official material, and I see online games and LARPs struggle with this as RPG rules that allow crafting generally allow far greater numbers of items than what is generally assumed to be in the game, and they all come up with different ways to handle it.<br /> <br /> At one point I did a census of Garou and their fetishes for NPCs listed in various Rage books. The different books seemed to be similar in how they assigned Fetishes, but this was an average only with plenty of exceptions. Rank 1 and 2 Garou typically did not have fetishes, but a small number of them had fetishes of any level (although it was typically low level fetishes of rank 1 or 2). Rank 3 Garou had one or more fetishes whose total level equaled their rank. This might mean one fetish of level 3, three level one fetishes, or one level 1 and one level 2 fetish. Since this is an average only, there were still Adren who had no fetishes and other Adren who had fetishes with greater combined levels, but not more than double their rank. Rank 4 or 5 Garou typically had 1 to 4 fetishes equal to their rank. Again this is average with some Garou having less or more than this. But even on the high level, these individual high ranked Garou did not seem to carry more levels in fetish than double their rank. So this is the guide I use for my own chronicles when creating NPCs and looking at the PCs to determine if they seem to be getting an appropriate amount of fetishes.<br /> <br /> Now character creation allows a PC to have multiple and powerful fetishes from the start. But as ST I warn players that other Garou can challenge other Garou to possess any fetish because they are more &quot;worthy&quot;. I get the general impression that fetishes are not truly &quot;owned&quot; by individual Garou, but by the Garou Nation as a whole and that individual Garou are more like &quot;trustees&quot; of a particular fetish. And trustees can change provided the appropriate challenges are met as required by Garou culture. A Cliath PC having a powerful and useful Rank 5 Fetish could easily lose it to an NPC. Of course, there can be various complications added to this - if a fetish is an heirloom belonging to a particular Garou lineage or tribe, other Garou might be more supportive of that Garou continuing to possess it. Or it might be technically held by a trusted Garou family member of family friend who keeps watch over it, but does &quot;lend&quot; it to the PC when needed. Or similar scenarios. But for the most part, a newly made PC with a powerful fetish is best encouraged to not let it be known that he has it.<br /> <br /> How often new fetishes are made if a more difficult problem because RPGs are notoriously bad at crafting rules. The rules are designed to give GMs and Players an easy guide to allow PCs to create whatever item and move on. They do not reflect any kind of economic model or reality that explains the actual number of items in the setting or provides a constraint to production. As written, almost all crafting rules in any RPG would allow massive overproduction of those crafted items in the setting. So Gamemasters are always needing a way to limit the creation of new items to a reasonable level. (Usually what I see in LARPs and Online games are an incredibly large number of successes to be achieved for planning, designing, crafting, etc. to create the fetish and then even more rolls to negotiate with the spirit. The idea being is to make the entire process so lengthy and not fun that it isn't abused. I personally don't enjoy this approach, but it seems to be popular perhaps because it can be applied easily to all players.)<br /> <br /> In my table top chronicles, I am currently requiring any fetish maker to adopt permanent changes to their behavior as a result of the chiminage pact to make a fetish. They might adopt certain &quot;bizarre&quot; taboos, geases, dress codes, body modifications,\u200b and so on that permanently alters how that character acts. (This is in addition to all the other chiminage to create a fetish described in various sourcebooks). I like it because it fits legends and is a good way to explain why people who deal a lot with the spirit world often act so strange. It also provides an incentive for characters to limit how many fetishes they create, because at some point it'll become very inconvenient and bothersome. One or a few such restraints might just lead to interesting quirks - the Garou who can't eat cooked food but must always eat it raw, and who also never wears colors that aren't earth tones; or a Garou who has carved various glyphs in his own skin and is forbidden to speak his own name; or a Garou who can never touch the dead or be near a menstruating woman. Or perhaps a Garou who must sleep among the dead, never harm felines of any nature, never cuts their hair or nails. Lots of interesting flavor text can be used. But eventually enough of these pile on that such quirks would become debilitating. It would prevent the Garou from living a relatively normal life (or PCs being able to do what they actually want to do during an RPG session).<br /> <br /> So I end up limiting those NPC Garou who create Fetishes to only create 3 -5 before they stop. Many Garou only create one fetish their entire lives, and many don't bother at all. It's a rare Garou who will go on creating more and more Fetishes because they don't mind how bizarre their actions will become.<br /> <br /> Instead, Talens become much more important.<br /> <br /> Because the number of games I run/play is limited, I really don't know how effective my house rule is at accomplishing the task I want it to (prevent a PC from mass producing fetishes). But it seems to be working so far. At the same time, I only ST tabletop with a select group of friends, and I doubt this system could work very well in large scale LARPs and Online games where I'd have to deal with a lot more twinks who want to game the system and it is harder for the ST to police and enforce it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-01-22T17:23:15-08:00","id":1501080,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1182_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1182\" data-vbnamecard=\"1182\">Asmodai</a> when you trade with the spirit to create the fetiche you must do something for him or accept a term with it if i understand. A week one could ask gnosis in exchange or a quick task before the ritual but some more powerfull would ask the garou to act in specific way after the fetiche creation.<br /> <br /> So let me think of a case: a music spirit would accept to be bound to a fetiche only if the garou asking for it is an artist traveler (a galliard silent strider with high performance level), and would ask the garou to prove his talent (by singing, play his instrument). Finally he could ask the garou to always help some kind of people, or always avoid some other, or never act in some way... of course the nature of these condition would depend of what kind of music spirit you're dealing with, a metal/rock one would ask different task than jazz one.<br /> If the garou is an arhoun fianna looking for a tool that could stun his ennemy to protect his cairn, it would be much difficult or even impossible to convince the spirit.<br /> <br /> So new question: as the spirit is sleeping when bounded in a fetiche, how can he sense what the garou is doing with him? The w20 state that unwilling spirit bounded in fetiche can result as a non working fetiche or badly, or turns against its user but without telling how the spirit sense he's badly used or taken by someone he does not want to be with.<br /> <br /> Thanks for the tips about how to represent these spirit anyway, will be useful soon<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar3046_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"3046\" data-vbnamecard=\"3046\">Black Fox</a> The crafting part is indeed a difficult aspect to manage, even without talking about magical artefact. In another aspect the wod books i have (v20, w20, m20) give the max weight a character according to their strength stat but not how to manage the clutter.<br /> <br /> Your system about ginving pc's some tasks, taboo or condition seems quite ideal to prevent the pc's to overcharge. I would use it in balance with some very hard chiminage or challenge before the fetiche creation (which could rely on poorly mastered or even unknown abilities of the werewolf to make his task really difficult), that way the st can have more solutions than just hinder the character<br /> <br /> Your census is quite surprising, i thought fetishes were much less common than this, but their power seems to be a key. High ranks are usually specialized in a certain category of action and use fetishes either to cover a weak point or to be even more effective, but since their responsibility can keep them away from the field they take fewer but more powerful fetishes, in the other hand pack leaders and rank 2/3 packs face more different situations and have more fetishes but less powerful and more varied. At least that's the impression I have\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26839,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","user":"Cain Loup-Noir","date":". 01-22-2023, 08:27 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}